CAT = "capswMental"; SEG = "MEN"
# VLO 14: mental health illness and challenges, cognitive and intellectual impairment, responsive behaviours.
Q = [
("What does Ontario long-term care call actions like hitting or wandering by a person with dementia?",
 "Responsive behaviours", ["Bad behaviours", "Wilful actions", "Disciplinary problems"],
 "\"Responsive behaviours\" reflects that the action responds to something, such as pain, fear or an unmet need. Finding the trigger is the first step."),
("A resident with dementia becomes agitated every afternoon. What is this often called?",
 "Sundowning", ["Delirium", "Psychosis", "Hallucinosis"],
 "Sundowning is increased confusion or agitation in the late afternoon and evening. Calm routines, good lighting and fewer demands can help."),
("How does delirium differ from dementia?",
 "It starts suddenly", ["It develops slowly over years", "It never affects attention", "It cannot be treated"],
 "Delirium comes on over hours to days, fluctuates and often has a treatable cause such as infection or medication. Dementia is gradual and progressive. Report sudden changes at once."),
("A resident with dementia insists she must go home to feed her children. What is the best response?",
 "Validate her and redirect", ["Tell her firmly they are grown", "Argue until she agrees with you", "Lock her in her room"],
 "Arguing with a person with dementia raises distress. Validate the feeling (\"you love your children\"), then gently redirect to a calming activity."),
("A resident is striking out during morning care. What should you do first?",
 "Stop, step back and stay calm", ["Hold her arms down firmly", "Finish care as fast as you can", "Raise your voice to stop her"],
 "Stop the task, give space and stay calm to keep everyone safe. Try again later or another way, and report the behaviour so triggers can be found."),
("Which might trigger a responsive behaviour?",
 "Pain or a full bladder", ["A calm, familiar room", "A familiar caregiver", "A regular routine"],
 "Unmet needs such as pain, hunger, a full bladder, being too hot or cold, noise or fear often trigger responsive behaviours. Familiar people and routines reduce them."),
("A client with schizophrenia says voices are telling him he is bad. What should you do?",
 "Acknowledge distress and report", ["Tell him the voices are not real", "Agree that the voices are right", "Ignore him until he stops"],
 "Do not argue about or agree with the hallucination. Acknowledge how upsetting it is, keep him safe and report it."),
("A client says, \"I won't be around much longer, so it doesn't matter.\" What should you do?",
 "Ask directly and report at once", ["Change the subject kindly", "Tell him to cheer up", "Mention it at next week's meeting"],
 "This may signal suicidal thoughts. Asking directly about suicide does not plant the idea. Stay with the client and report immediately to the nurse or supervisor."),
("Which is a common sign of depression in an older adult?",
 "Losing interest in hobbies", ["Laughing more than usual", "Eating more at every single meal", "Making many new friends"],
 "Depression often shows as loss of interest, withdrawal, sleep and appetite changes and sadness. It is not a normal part of ageing and should be reported."),
("A client with anxiety starts breathing fast during care. What helps most?",
 "Breathing slowly with her", ["Telling her to stop it now", "Leaving her alone quickly", "Talking about her fears loudly"],
 "Staying calm, speaking softly and breathing slowly with the client can ease panic. Report the episode."),
("A person with moderate dementia keeps asking what day it is. What is the best reply?",
 "Answer simply each time", ["Remind her she already asked", "Ignore the question", "Give her a quiz on dates"],
 "Short-term memory loss means she does not remember asking. A calm, simple answer avoids embarrassment and distress."),
("A resident wanders into other residents' rooms. Which approach is best?",
 "Find the reason for it", ["Restrain her in a chair", "Scold her each time she does it", "Keep her in bed all day"],
 "Wandering often has a purpose, such as looking for the toilet or boredom. Meet the need and provide safe places to walk; restraint is a last resort."),
("Which is true of a client with an intellectual disability?",
 "Learning may take more time", ["They cannot make any choices", "They should be treated like children", "They never live independently"],
 "People with intellectual disabilities learn best with simple steps, repetition and patience. They have the same rights to make choices and be treated as adults."),
("A client who uses substances is withdrawn and shaky after you arrive. What should you do?",
 "Observe and report it", ["Lecture him about his choices", "Search his room for drugs right away", "Refuse to give care"],
 "Shaking and agitation may be withdrawal, which can be dangerous. Report observations without judgement and follow the plan of care."),
("Which communication approach helps a person in later-stage dementia?",
 "One step at a time", ["Long explanations of the task", "Several questions at once", "Speaking from behind them"],
 "Simple words, one-step instructions, eye contact from the front and gestures help a person with dementia understand. Approaching from behind can startle."),
("What is reminiscence used for with older adults?",
 "Sharing memories", ["Testing memory for a diagnosis", "Correcting false memories", "Teaching new skills fast"],
 "Reminiscence invites people to share past experiences, which supports self-worth and connection. It is not a memory test."),
]
